package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	var n int
	fmt.Scan(&n)
	prices := make([]float64, n)
	for i := range prices {
		fmt.Scan(&prices[i])
	}
	sum := 0.0
	for _, p := range prices {
		sum += p
	}
	avg := sum / float64(n)
	above := 0
	for _, p := range prices {
		if p > avg {
			above++
		}
	}
	fmt.Printf("Average: %.2f\n", avg)
	fmt.Println("Above average:", above)
}

steps.body:
Often you do not know how many values are coming. Keep calling `fmt.Scan` until it fails: at the end of input it returns the error `io.EOF`.

```go
for {
	var word string
	if _, err := fmt.Scan(&word); err != nil {
		break // no more input
	}
	// use word
}
```

Scanning into a `string` reads one word: everything up to the next space or newline. So words spread over several lines arrive one at a time.

The same loop reads records of several values. `fmt.Scan(&name, &score)` fills both variables in order, each converted to its own type, and returns an error once the input runs out.

Watch out: when you scan into an int, a token that is not a number also returns an error, and this loop stops early. Do not mix `fmt.Scan` and a `bufio.Scanner` in one program either. The scanner reads ahead into its buffer, so `fmt.Scan` would miss that input.

Input is a natural fit for maps and sets. Count with `counts[w]++` or collect with `seen[w] = struct{}{}`. Map order is random, so collect the keys, `sort.Strings` them, and print in that order.

example.code:
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sort"
	"strings"
)

func main() {
	counts := make(map[string]int)
	for {
		var word string
		if _, err := fmt.Scan(&word); err != nil {
			break // io.EOF: no more words
		}
		counts[strings.ToLower(word)]++
	}
	words := make([]string, 0, len(counts))
	for w := range counts {
		words = append(words, w)
	}
	sort.Strings(words)
	for _, w := range words {
		fmt.Println(w, counts[w])
	}
}

